Z||000000||lennyb4||04-25-2000||08:45 AM||||Ive been checking around up here (cleveland, area) and the cheapest clutch install i can find is a min. of $700 total. Now i can get the parts for around $200. Im going to do it myself, cant see paying that much. I've done clutches on other cars but never a fiero. I know i can do it, im just wondering how big of a pain in the butt is it? (one. two ,three days??)||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||04-25-2000||08:49 AM||||I would say the toughest part of the whole job is getting that darned exhaust crossover pipe off. <P>If you don't run into many problems(and there aren't many frozen bolts), it is possible to do it in a day. I would tend to lean towards 3 days just in case you run into problems.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||04-25-2000||10:12 AM||||Seems like I hear lots of cussing about cradle bolts too. The guys that can do this job in a day or two have probably done it before. if you're like me, anything that can go wrong, will. Plan accordingly. Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||04-25-2000||11:33 AM||||You don't mention if it is a 4 or V6. I have an 88 Formula. I took off the rear (back of car) cradle bolts and rocked the cradle/engine down to acces the transmission from the wheel opening. You need to disconnect the whole suspension out of the way and lift the rear of the car very high. The first time I lowered it with a friend and put everything back myself. Can't recall the time spent for that one. But with that experience I did it a second time all alone. Not even my wife helped me out. It took me one weekend to take all apart (+/- 8 hrs each day Sat & Sun) and one weekend to put all back and bleed it (8 hrs Sat & 6 hrs Sun). With constant help from a friend you may be able to do it in two long days. Also I have to mention that I did not rush. Didn't wanna miss a part or break something. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
